# Public-only review of option metadata amendment r1

Actual reviewer `/root/research_architect`, 2026-10-10 UTC. Conclusion: **earlier critique substantially resolved; revise the optional canonical member-hash provision before production**. The semantic policy,12 proposed source indices,8/4 split, nine contexts and216/108/300/1,944 counts remain unchanged. No candidate, private checker, producer source, test gold or other review judgment was inspected; this is public design review with zero data approval.

Exact amendment SHA256 `5f7f6a2c45dd6a0b51c7a912b28fd49badd9aa3aa2eac8632e9b9781c201b57b` (12,353 bytes), root freeze `3ac64a15a6dcb07a06831b5a180239672ce0394bfaeb90f7d04e473baba42293`. Preserved original metadata1612, root freezeab948, task contract4465, configuration422f, curriculum8bda and prior actual review4a667 remain exact bindings in this review's preexecution receipt. No old file is edited.

## Resolved points

- The operative container is `family_control_claims`; a parallel ignored alias is excluded. Names are normalized from `name` or `property`, with both allowed only if equal. Typed optional owner coordinates and diagnostics are now explicit. This is a clear prospective vocabulary, rather than an unpublished closed-key assumption. Later integration tests must exercise both permitted identifiers and every optional form through the actual generic canonical validator as well as the isolated cohort checker; public-text consistency alone does not prove that integration.
- Exact five-source path-to-SHA bindings, manifest-version/batch/candidate fields and the named-entry resolver are now published. Manifest SHA is externally frozen rather than embedded in candidate claims. Extra manifest diagnostics do not become mandatory. Claims resolve actual ordered IDs by identifier and control ID; shared descriptive IDs and nonmember owners remain legitimate.
- The field-order owner exception expressly matches family/index/split/context, with members forward/reverse. Same-renderer matching applies only to the first eight names. This avoids applying an impossible common-renderer requirement to a field-order pair.
- Empty optional property witnesses are allowed. Typed locator/metric keys and narrative diagnostics are defined; no invented row-property vocabulary or mandatory annotation is inferred. Difficulty is2..4 with actual dependency explanation and independently derived evidence, not count-based inflation.
- Retained child initiation, selected branch, caller work, register-derived bindings, I/S decisive atoms and pending return work are now explicit. P may leave a return rather than an additional effect, which agrees with the original effect/return wording. X diagnostics distinguish the actually read retained frame copy, cursor selection and active state from inactive duplicates; invalid checkpoints are never accepted via the diagnostic continuation.
- Replay is tied to actual declared instruction locators and committed/new traces. Counter reset and changed after-state do not invalidate a real replay. The g(g(s)) versus g(s) condition explicitly excludes zero additions, identity/idempotent scaling and equal-operand swaps; actual repeated execution and terminal/trace difference remain required.
- Full-option and two shared-primitive graph views are now published. Reachable and all-declared views prevent counting unused padding as fresh families. Literal/name/context/cut changes do not establish novelty; actual12-program and legacy comparisons remain pending, not proven by a feasible skeleton.

The universal selected-branch guard-reversal obligation and three-view collision gate are now explicit prospective cohort requirements. They strengthen the plain original first-cut/normalization descriptions without changing primitive interpreter semantics. The checker should attribute these precise requirements to the amendment, rather than claim their full wording was already present in the old first-cut sentence. No existing data is affected because none has been produced under this interface.

## Remaining circular member-hash issue

The claim-vocabulary section permits optional `pair_record_hashes` inside a canonical record, and requires those hashes to equal the actual full resolved-member hashes. For `object_field_order_invariance`, the owner must itself be one of the two members. Under the established full-record hashing rule, excluding only the top-level `record_hash`, putting the owner's own full hash inside one of its claims creates the equation h = SHA256(record containing h). This requires a cryptographic fixed point, rather than ordinary acyclic content-addressing. A first-eight claim owned by a member has the same issue; reciprocal member references can also create cycles. This is the same class of custody feedback as the manifest/candidate cycle correctly prohibited elsewhere in this amendment.

This observation does not prove that no cryptographic fixed point mathematically exists. It means the public interface must not require or advertise ordinary generation of such a self-consistent field without a separately defined hashing exemption or acyclic construction. No exemption excluding nested claim hashes has been published, and inventing one would alter the established full-record custody rule. The mandatory cohort can omit this optional field, so the semantic nine-context design remains feasible; the advertised optional form is unsafe for member owners.

Prefer a NEW prospective correction removing `pair_record_hashes` from canonical claims while retaining optional member hashes in external manifest entries or separately frozen evidence. Candidate claims contain ordered pair IDs; calculate complete record hashes, then the complete candidate hash, then assemble/freeze a manifest containing those hashes. The checker independently verifies actual member hashes whether a manifest annotation is supplied or absent. Preserve amendment5f7f/root freeze3ac6 and all initial checker failures. A more complex alternative can allow canonical hash references only under a publicly proved acyclic dependency graph, but it adds no useful semantic coverage here.

The small hand checks accompanying this review inspect only abstract hash-dependency graphs: a member-owner reference has a self-cycle, two cross references have a cycle, a nonmember referring to two leaf members can be acyclic, and an external manifest consuming candidate/member hashes has no feedback into candidate records. They are not a task interpreter, corpus checker or candidate proof.

## Boundary and later gates

The nine-context simultaneous feasibility argument from the first review remains valid in principle, including a branch whose guard reverses and a child initiation that is no longer true while root initiation still passes. Source novelty under all three views, every actual control, diagnostic activity and all numeric bounds still need independently executed preproduction evidence. The augmented optional vocabulary also needs actual generic-validator/cohort-checker integration tests before a candidate can rely on it; I inspected no implementation and issue no code verdict.

I authored the original task/curriculum/config and may later adjudicate; this public review has design/review overlap. Root supplied the amendment, prior pipeline-container finding and a summary of formal read-only concerns; the formal engineer's private work was not read. Same-model/common-guidance risks, feasibility reuse, exposed dev and finite-program shortcuts remain. Nothing here establishes learned hierarchy, model efficacy, sealed transfer or data quality.
